Using the distributive and anticommutative properties of the cross product:
\begin{align*}
(5 \bold{a} + 7 \bold{b}) \times (-\bold{a} + 3 \bold{b}) &= 5\bold{a} \times (-\bold{a}) + 5\bold{a} \times (3\bold{b}) + 7\bold{b} \times (-\bold{a}) + 7\bold{b} \times (3\bold{b}) \\
&= -5(\bold{a} \times \bold{a}) + 15(\bold{a} \times \bold{b}) - 7(\bold{b} \times \bold{a}) + 21(\bold{b} \times \bold{b})
\end{align*}
Since $\bold{a} \times \bold{a} = \bold{b} \times \bold{b} = \bold{0}$ and $\bold{b} \times \bold{a} = -(\bold{a} \times \bold{b})$:
\begin{align*}
(5 \bold{a} + 7 \bold{b}) \times (-\bold{a} + 3 \bold{b}) &= 15(\bold{a} \times \bold{b}) + 7(\bold{a} \times \bold{b}) \\
&= 22(\bold{a} \times \bold{b})
\end{align*}
Thus, $k = 22$.

